                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   So we split it, the way adults do who love each other and don’t
agree. She would build the whole file: spec, ledger, the Calm Index, the
permits, Vale. She would not publish a word until we knew what June
thirteenth was. And she wanted to meet, in her own words, “the old
man with the drums, and everyone else who’s in on it, because I’m not
joining a magic war, I’m joining a source meeting.”
   “You’re going to hate the dinner,” Manny said.
   “What dinner?”
   “My mom’s?” I said. I hadn’t decided it until I said it. “She’s cooking
for everyone. She says nobody saves a borough on an empty stomach.”



Mamá cooked for two days. She would not be talked out of it. By the
time the guests arrived, the apartment smelled like every important
day of my life at once: pernil, moro de guandules, tostones, ensalada
rusa, a flan the size of a hubcap.
   I hadn’t known what a council of guardians looked like. I’d half ex‐
pected robes.
   Rafa came first, in his good shirt, awkward indoors, holding a bag of
mangoes and apologizing to the ceiling. Yenni came after him, straight
from the daycare, still wearing a sticker that said I WAS BRAVE. Mama
Tete arrived last, broad and unhurried, with a presence that made the
kitchen rearrange itself around her, a covered dish in one hand and a
look in the other that went around the room and settled on Sofía like a
gavel.
   “Who is this?” she said.
   “Sofía. Paper.”
   “Paper.” Mama Tete’s eyebrows went up. “I don’t speak to paper.”
   “I don’t speak to drums,” said Sofía, pleasantly. “I only read them.”
   There was a silence that could have curdled milk. Mamá, without
turning from the stove, said in her most reasonable voice, “She eats.”
   That was all. Mama Tete looked at my mother’s back, and my moth‐
er looked at the pernil, and some treaty was signed that I wasn’t in‐
vited to read.




